WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:06.080
One of the needs we may have when running queries&nbsp;
is to find records where certain values are empty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.280
whether by design or by accident. Take, for&nbsp;
example, this query that has some header&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.080
information for sales orders. There is a rumor&nbsp;
that some orders may be missing a SalesPersonID.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:26.320
I will add a WHERE clause to this query that&nbsp;
looks for empty values in the SalesPersonID&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:33.040
field to find those orders. There are two keys&nbsp;
to understand. First, the keyword for empty is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:41.120
NULL. Secondly, we do not want to use equals NULL&nbsp;
because technically, we would look for the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:48.880
NULL. We do not want that. We are looking to see&nbsp;
if a field is null, and that is what we will type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:58.720
here. I will execute this, and it turns out over&nbsp;
60000 orders do not have a SalesPersonID. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.400
there is a huge data entry problem, or this is&nbsp;
by design, like a cash sale or something similar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.320
To compare orders with no SalesPersonID&nbsp;
with orders that do have one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:17.360
I will change the criteria from NULL&nbsp;
to NOT NULL and execute this again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.040
and we have about 60000 orders with a&nbsp;
SalesPersonID. Therefore, this was probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:32.960
by design. The takeaway here is that the NULL&nbsp;
keyword will help us find those empty values.

